    {
      "id": "assumptions_categorized",
      "category": "Data Quality",
      "label": "Assumptions categorized",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "Every assumption carries a category label: 'sourced' (with citation), 'derived' (with stated factors and formula), or 'agent estimate' (explicitly flagged). The 2% capture rate is the sole agent estimate and is clearly identified as such. No assumption is left unlabeled."
    },
    {
      "id": "both_approaches_used",
      "category": "Methodology",
      "label": "Both approaches used",
      "status": "pass",
      "notes": "Both top-down and bottom-up approaches are used. The top-down anchors to industry FSM market reports and applies segment fractions. The bottom-up builds from US Census establishment counts multiplied by a blended ARPU. Both produce TAM, SAM, and SOM estimates that are compared directly."
    },
    {
      "id": "approaches_reconciled",
      "category": "Methodology",
      "label": "Gap between the two approaches is explained",
      "status": "fail",
      "notes": "The TAM estimates agree closely (0.2% gap), but the SAM and SOM estimates differ by a factor of 2.0 \u2014 the bottom-up SAM is $804M versus the top-down SAM of $402M. The analysis flags this divergence but does not explain what drives it. The gap arises because the top-down applies a revenue-based SMB fraction (46%) to a revenue-based trade-contractor market, while the bottom-up applies an establishment-count-based SMB fraction (91.8%) to an establishment count \u2014 two different bases measuring different things. This explanation is absent from the analysis and must be added before presenting to investors."
